// ============================================================================
// CONFIG-RESOLUTION · KNOBS  (framework name lists — the only per-ecosystem edit)
//
// The counterpart of java/engine/config-resolution/knobs.dl, in the matching phase,
// and for the same reason:
// every framework-specific NAME lives here and nowhere else, so adding an ecosystem is
// a matter of adding facts, and NO RULE MENTIONS A FRAMEWORK BY NAME.
//
// WHY NOT resolution/builtins.dl, where this list first landed. That file is about
// CPython's C-level callables — `super`, `object`, `len` — things with no Python source
// anywhere. An HTTP verb is not a builtin; it is a name a third-party framework chose.
// Keeping the two apart is what makes "which names did we assume?" answerable in one
// file per language, which is the whole point of the Java convention.
// ============================================================================

// ── py_http_route_verb(Verb) — the HTTP method a route decorator is named after ──
// A WSGI/ASGI framework registers a handler by decorating it with the HTTP verb:
// `@app.route(...)` (Flask, Bottle), `@router.get(...)` / `@app.post(...)` (FastAPI,
// Starlette), `@routes.get(...)` (an aiohttp route table), `@api.websocket(...)`.
//
// The set is HTTP's, not any project's — RFC 9110's methods plus `route` (a
// verb-agnostic registration) and `websocket` (the upgrade handshake) — so it
// generalises by construction rather than by sampling, and it did: it matched a third
// framework it was never written against, unmodified.
//
// A decorator is a route ONLY if its final segment is NAMED here, so the list can move
// a declaration from "no entry point" to "entry point" and never silently the other way.
//
// THE NAME IS NOT SUFFICIENT AND IS NEVER MATCHED ALONE. `patch` is an HTTP method and
// also the stdlib's mock entry point, so `@mock.patch(...)` satisfies <receiver>.<verb>
// exactly — measured, that was 229 of 253 matches across five real projects.
// config-resolution/entry-points.dl also requires a URL path argument, which a mock target,
// being a dotted name, never has.
py_http_route_verb("route").
py_http_route_verb("get").
py_http_route_verb("post").
py_http_route_verb("put").
py_http_route_verb("delete").
py_http_route_verb("patch").
py_http_route_verb("head").
py_http_route_verb("options").
py_http_route_verb("websocket").

// ── py_grpc_servicer_suffix(Suffix) / py_grpc_generated_module_suffix(Suffix) ──
// The two names protoc chooses, and the only two this ecosystem needs.
//
// `grpc_python_plugin` writes one module per `.proto`, named `<stem>_pb2_grpc.py`, and in
// it one abstract base per service, named `<Service>Servicer`. Both spellings are the
// plugin's, not a project's: they are produced by the code generator, so a codebase cannot
// choose them differently and a new gRPC project needs no addition here.
//
// NEITHER IS SUFFICIENT ALONE, and config-resolution/entry-points.dl requires both. A class
// called `OrderServicer` in ordinary hand-written source is not a gRPC servicer, and a
// module suffix on its own says nothing about which classes in it are service bases. The
// pair is what makes the match provenance-based rather than name-based.
py_grpc_servicer_suffix("Servicer").
py_grpc_generated_module_suffix("_pb2_grpc.py").
